# The FTP account on the shared host is chrooted to the web root, so this
# Laravel project physically lives INSIDE the document root — unlike Laravel's
# normal deployment, where everything but public/ sits outside it. Deny direct
# web access to the whole tree so .env, vendor/ and app/ are unreachable even
# though they are physically web-accessible; public/.htaccess grants access
# back for the one subdirectory that is meant to be reachable.
#
# This is the load-bearing line, not a second layer behind the front-controller
# catch-all. Authorization is evaluated during Apache's directory walk, BEFORE
# mod_rewrite's per-directory rules run in the fixup phase — so the catch-all in
# app/.htaccess never gets the chance to rewrite a request that resolves under
# this tree. Verified against the local stack: /api-laravel/.env,
# /api-laravel/vendor/autoload.php and even a nonexistent path all return 403
# (authz_core AH01630), not the old app's 404.
#
# Which makes the NOTE below the important one: this file reaches no server.
#
# NOTE: this file does NOT currently reach any server. The deploy CLI treats
# ".htaccess" as a protected basename at ANY depth (the PROTECTED set in
# tools/deploy/preflight.mjs, applied by walkBuild in local.mjs and by the
# stale-file filters in sync.mjs), so it is filtered out of every upload even
# though tools/build.mjs copies it into dist/build/. The boundary below is
# therefore live only in the local Docker stack. Making the protected set
# root-relative is a prerequisite for sub-project 2a-ii — until then,
# dispatching /api/* on a real server would expose .env and vendor/.
#
# Deliberately no line numbers here: this file outlives them. The dated
# implementation plan carries the precise citations.
#
# The host's Apache version is NOT established by anything in this repo, so
# both spellings stay guarded. staging/README.md's note that the host 500s on
# <RequireAny> leans 2.2 (it is a valid container on 2.4, and AuthConfig
# override is granted here). The unguarded "Require all denied" in
# staging/test/.htaccess proves nothing to the contrary: Require is RAW_ARGS on
# 2.2 so it parses there too, and that line sits in a <FilesMatch> the host's
# stock ^\.ht deny already covers, so it never runs. Do NOT simplify the guard
# away — on 2.2 an unguarded "Require all granted" is an unrecognized entity
# and yields 401/500 instead of a grant.
<IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    Require all denied
</IfModule>
<IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    Deny from all
</IfModule>
